  "releaseNotes": "Containers that hold things by shape. A slot count asks a player how many things they are carrying; a\ngrid asks what shape those things are, and a rifle that eats half a bag is a decision about whether to\ntake it.\n\n### Added\n\n- `GridContainerModel`: a rectangle of cells items occupy by footprint. They turn a quarter when that\n  is the only way they fit, and are refused when they do not — a container that quietly drops what does\n  not fit is one a player cannot plan against, and on an authoritative server it is a client and a\n  server disagreeing about what came out of a raid. A whole stack takes the footprint of one of them,\n  and stacks already inside are topped up before a new one is opened.\n- `GridContainerDefinition`, `GridPlacement` and `GridContainerSnapshot`, with the snapshot registered\n  for serialisation.\n- `ItemDefinition.GridWidth`, `GridHeight` and `Container`. All defaulted, so a game with no grid\n  anywhere sets none of them and nothing changes. Validation refuses a footprint of zero — an item with\n  no size can be carried without limit — and a container on an item that names no slot, which is one\n  nothing could ever open.\n- `OwnedItemsSnapshot.Containers` and `SlotContainerSnapshot`: what is inside each equipped container,\n  keyed by slot rather than by the container's instance id, so swapping one bag for another of the same\n  size keeps its contents.\n\nSizes are resolved through the catalog rather than saved, for the same reason nothing else here saves\nan item's stats: a bag stored last week must reflect a rifle resized this morning. Only positions are\nsaved. An item that grew since it was stored is moved rather than lost, and dropped only when the bag\ngenuinely cannot hold it.",
